  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. Compliance at Khalifa: VAT, duty, free zones and Abu Dhabi clearance</h2>

    <h3>Tax and duty</h3>
    <p>
      UAE import VAT is <strong>5%</strong>, effective since 1 January 2018, and is calculated on the
      CIF value <em>plus</em> customs duty. The baseline import duty is <strong>5% of CIF</strong>
      under the GCC Common External Tariff, with higher duty or excise treatment possible on specific
      goods such as tobacco, alcohol and motor vehicles.
    </p>

    <h3>Clearance in the correct order</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Classify the goods with the correct HS code before booking.</li>
      <li>Confirm importer eligibility and any product-specific permits.</li>
      <li>Prepare the commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading and certificate of origin so every line agrees.</li>
      <li>File the pre-arrival declaration through the Abu Dhabi customs authority — confirm the current electronic system with AD Ports or your broker, since Dubai’s Mirsal 2 is specific to Dubai.</li>
      <li>Pay the assessed duty and VAT, and complete any inspection, to obtain release.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Documents, classification and KIZAD free zones</h3>
    <p>
      Standard documents are the commercial invoice, bill of lading, packing list and certificate of
      origin. Classify goods with the correct <strong>HS code</strong> before quoting, because both
      duty and any permit requirements depend on it. Note that <strong>ISF</strong> is a United States
      requirement and does not apply to the UAE.
    </p>
    <p>
      For goods destined to KIZAD or another Abu Dhabi free zone, confirm whether the consignee will
      hold the cargo inside the zone under duty/VAT suspension or move it to the mainland. The answer
      changes the duty/VAT line and the clearance authority used.
    </p>

    <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — UAE customs, VAT & Abu Dhabi ports" />
